    "result_text": "Done. Here's how I addressed the review:\n\n**Duplicated SHA-256 hex helper (agreed, fixed).** Consolidated to a single primitive: `sha256HexPrefix(value, hexChars)` (lower-case) now does all the digest-to-hex work, and `sha256Hex8` is expressed in terms of it \u2014 `sha256HexPrefix(value, 8).toUpperCase()`. The customer-code contract (upper-case, 8 chars = first 4 bytes) is unchanged, and there's no longer a parallel copy of the `MessageDigest`/loop/exception-handling to drift.\n\n**`assignPossibleDuplicate` collapsed to two setters (noted, left as-is).** The reviewer flagged this as minor and non-blocking, and explicitly agreed keeping it is defensible. I concur: it preserves the uniform step-per-method shape of `createOwner` and carries the javadoc explaining *why* the flags are always cleared under the new household rule (declared members aren't suspected duplicates; any other same-household owner is already rejected). Inlining it would move that rationale away from the create flow, so I left it.\n\nTests remain green: BUILD SUCCESS, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
